markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Parallel Arrays

Once you have a schema, one way to store the data in JavaScript is with **parallel arrays**. Each array represents one “column” from the schema, and the same index across arrays refers to the same item.


<div style="background-color: #B7E0E5; border-radius: 10px; padding: 15px; margin: 20px auto; max-width: 85%; box-sizing: border-box; word-wrap: break-word; overflow-wrap: break-word;">
<p style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 12px; line-height: 120%;"> 📖
<strong>Parallel arrays-</strong> multiple arrays that stay “in sync” with each other
</p>
</div>

---
## Parallel Arrays in a Music App

<img src="https://images.code.org/e3815e7ed54603e735e4cfb18bdef643-image-1765393090426.png" alt="code for arrays for the shcema in a music app- title, artist, length, favorite" width="650px" style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;border-radius:10px; ">

**Here’s how to read this:**

All of the values at index 0 describe the first song:

- songTitles[0] → "Golden Hour"

- songArtists[0] → "JVKE"

- songLengths[0] → 210

- songIsFavorite[0] → true

All of the values at index 1 describe the second song, and so on.

We call these **parallel arrays** because they run side-by-side and stay lined up by index. Each index acts like a “row” in a table.

---

## Why Schema and Parallel Arrays Matter in Your Teaching

<div style="background-color:#f7f8fa; border-radius: 10px; padding: 15px; margin: 20px auto; max-width: 95%; box-sizing: border-box; word-wrap: break-word; overflow-wrap: break-word;"">
  <img 
    src="https://images.code.org/fe4fef3adb3a958ff0fc5afe01bdccc1-image-1765393562055.png" 
    alt="icon of hand with hovering lightbulb" 
    width="120px"
    style="border-radius:10px; float:left; margin: 10px 30px 40px 20px;"
  >

  Using schema and parallel arrays helps students see data as organized and meaningful, not just a list of random values.

  It also connects their work to real apps they recognize and gives them practice keeping data aligned across arrays.
</div>

<div style="clear:both;"></div>

---


## 🔍 Check for Understandng
> #### Before moving on, define the following term in your own words: **parallel arrays**.
> - #### What does it mean for parallel arrays to stay “in sync” by index?


<details> <summary style="cursor:pointer;display:inline-block;background:#8148B2;color:white;padding:14px 22px;font-size:18px;font-weight:600;border-radius:999px;list-style:none;"> Check your thinking </summary> <div style="margin-top:16px; background:#f7f8fa;"> <p style = "font-size: 14pt;"><b>Parallel arrays</b> are multiple arrays that store related data separately but correspond to each other.</p> <p style = "font-size: 14pt;">Staying “in sync” means that values at the same index across all arrays refer to the same item or record.</p> <p style = "font-size: 14pt;">Each index acts like a row of data, connecting information across the arrays.</p> </div> </details>
MARKDOWN
